Revisions and Backfills
Vintage-aware research must also record which version of a value was used.
The FRED series/vintagedates endpoint identifies dates when a series value was revised or new values were released (Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, n.d.-d). A later snapshot can differ from an earlier one because of:

routine revisions;
benchmark or seasonal-adjustment revisions;
historical backfills;
source methodology changes; or
changes in the set of available series.

A reproducible pipeline should retain enough metadata to distinguish these possibilities rather than merely store the final numeric matrix.

Dependence diagnostics
A multivariate forecast should also be challenged on the structure connecting its components. Useful diagnostics include:

Variogram Score;
forecast-versus-realized covariance or correlation patterns;
rank correlation;
co-exceedance frequencies in adverse tails;
conditional cross-asset behavior during large moves; and
portfolio projections that are sensitive to diversification breakdown.

Some of these are descriptive diagnostics rather than proper scores. That distinction should be stated.

Simulation stability
A taxonomy that changes dramatically when only the random-number seed or simulation count changes may be too noisy for stable interpretation.
Useful checks include:

family-share dispersion across independent RNG seeds;
representative-scenario distance across seeds;
ranking stability of dominant families;
convergence as the number of simulated paths \(M\) increases; and
sensitivity to reasonable tail thresholds.

A fixed seed is useful for reproducibility, but seed reproducibility is not Monte Carlo stability. Stability requires repeating the analysis across seeds or larger simulation clouds.

Taxonomy adequacy
A rule set should be audited for more than label completeness. Useful diagnostics include:

raw rule overlap before exclusivity is imposed;
residual Other share;
very small named families that cannot be estimated stably;
within-family dispersion in standardized geometry; and
sensitivity of family assignments to small threshold changes.

A taxonomy can be exhaustive by construction yet economically poor if most scenarios fall into Other or if named families contain highly heterogeneous geometries.
